The system context is the known glitch. The `<new_project>` is a glitch — this is the existing IVX Holdings project. The `plan_md` is the completed 16-phase certification. The `current_mode` is correct (Agent Mode).

I have one failing test. The issue: the test expects a transient failure at step 2 to result in "paused" status, but it's getting "deadlettered". Let me look at why.

The test creates a job with `maxAttempts: 3`. It arms a failure injection at step 2 with `failureClass: 'transient'`. Then calls `executeWithRecovery`.

In `executeWithRecovery`, when the injected failure is thrown, the error message is set to `injection.errorMessage` which is "Injected transient failure at step 2" — but the `classifyFailure` function doesn't recognize this as transient because it doesn't contain "timeout", "network", etc. keywords.

Wait, let me look at the code more carefully. In `executeWithRecovery`:

```typescript
if (injection && injection.armed && step === injection.failAtStep) {
  const error = new Error(injection.errorMessage);
  (error as Error & { failureClass?: FailureClass }).failureClass = injection.failureClass;
  if (injection.failureClass === 'permanent') {
    error.message = `[permanent] ${injection.errorMessage}`;
  }
  throw error;
}
```

So for transient, the error message is just "Injected transient failure at step 2". Then `reportFailure` calls `classifyFailure(error)`, which checks the message for keywords like "timeout", "network", etc. "Injected transient failure at step 2" doesn't match any of those, so it returns 'unknown'.

Then in `reportFailure`:
```typescript
